    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Is this a good offer?\n\n**In isolation, it is a standard, median Google L6 offer.** However, **for your specific situation, it is not a compelling offer to accept as-is.**\n\nHere is why:\n\n* **Slight Base Pay Cut:** Your base would drop from **$240,000 to $235,000**. Google\u2019s posted band goes up to **$271,000**, meaning there is substantial headroom.\n* **Marginal Year 1 Upside (~5% bump):** \n  * Meta: $240k (base) + $36k (bonus) + $250k (stock) = **~$526,000**\n  * Google (assuming standard 25% annualized equity): $235k (base) + $47k (bonus) + $270k (stock) = **~$552,000**\n  * Leaving a job where you are happy, proven, and established for a ~$26k (~5%) pre-tax difference is essentially a lateral move with transition risk.\n* **Missing Sign-on Bonus:** Google offered **$0 sign-on**, despite you forfeiting $200k in near-term vesting Meta equity and potential upcoming bonus cycles.\n* **The \"Refresh Stacking\" Argument is Flawed:** Recruiters often highlight Google's stacking refreshes to make Year 3 and Year 4 numbers look large. However:\n  * Meta *also* awards annual stacking refreshes for IC6 PMs.\n  * You already have an active refresh pipeline at Meta. Switching resets your internal capital and performance history.\n  * Future refreshes are performance-dependent and never guaranteed.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strategy: Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n**You should negotiate firmly.**\n\nBecause you were passively recruited and are happy at Meta, you hold **maximum leverage**. If Google meets your numbers, you get a substantial step up in compensation; if they refuse, you happily stay put.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For and Specific Targets\n\nGoogle\u2019s initial offer leaves room across every component. You should aim to bring **Year 1 Total Compensation to ~$650,000+**.\n\n| Component | Current Meta | Google Initial Offer | Your Counter-Target | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Puts you above your current base while staying inside their $183k\u2013$271k band. |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **$51,000 \u2013 $52,000** | Automatically scales with the higher base (20%). |\n| **Equity (4-yr)** | $250k/yr | $1.08M ($270k/yr) | **$1.3M \u2013 $1.4M** (~$325k\u2013$350k/yr) | L6 PM equity at Google frequently caps out in this range for competitive external hires. |\n| **Sign-on Bonus**| N/A | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Standard tool Google uses to bridge unvested equity and upcoming bonuses. |\n| **Year 1 TC** | **$526,000** | **$552,000** | **~$700,000+ (incl. sign-on)** | Meaningful premium to justify the move. |\n\n*(Note: Verify Google's specific vesting cadence for your offer. Google frequently uses front-loaded vesting schedules such as 33/33/22/12% or 38/32/20/10% rather than straight 25%/yr. If front-loaded, Year 1 stock value is naturally higher, but the overall 4-year grant size should still be negotiated up.)*\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Frame the Negotiation (Script / Template)\n\nSend an email or use these talking points on your next call with the recruiter:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m genuinely excited about the scope of the role.*\n>\n> *As mentioned when we first connected, I wasn\u2019t actively looking and am very happy with my current scope, team, and trajectory at Meta. When I look at the financial package side-by-side, the current offer is essentially lateral to what I am making today ($526k total comp, with a higher base salary and substantial unvested equity over the next 12 months).*\n>\n> *For me to leave a role where I\u2019m performing well and take on the friction of a transition, the compensation needs to provide a clear step forward. To make this an easy decision, I am looking for:*\n> \n> 1. * **Base Salary:** $255,000 (to ensure I\u2019m not taking a base reduction)*\n> 2. * **Equity:** $1.35M over 4 years*\n> 3. * **Sign-on Bonus:** $80,000 (to offset walking away from my near-term unvested Meta RSUs and bonus timing)*\n>\n> *If we can get the package to this level, I would be thrilled to sign and commit right away.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Decision Matrix\n\n* **If they meet all or most of your asks (Year 1 TC \u2265 $630k+ with sign-on/equity bumps):** **Accept**, provided you like the specific Google team and scope.\n* **If they make minor adjustments (e.g., small bump to $570k with no sign-on):** **Walk away.** A 5\u20138% increase does not justify the execution risk or the loss of momentum at Meta.",
