    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Numbers Breakdown\n\nGoogle gave you a major concession by adding a **$200,000 sign-on bonus** and matching your Meta base at **$240,000**. \n\nHere is how the side-by-side looks:\n\n| Component | Meta (Status Quo) | Google Revised Offer | Difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $0 |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $48,000 (20%) | +$12,000 |\n| **Annual Equity (Grant)** | ~$250,000 | $270,000 *(even 25% split)*<br>or ~$356,000 *(front-loaded 33%)* | +$20,000 to +$106,000 |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | $200,000 | +$200,000 |\n| **Year 1 Total Comp** | **~$526,000** | **$758,000 \u2013 $844,000** | **+$232,000 to +$318,000 (~44\u201360% bump)** |\n\n---\n\n### 2. Year 2 and Beyond: The \u201cSign-On Cliff\u201d Reality\n\nWhile Year 1 is exceptionally strong, understand how Year 2 and Year 3 will look once the sign-on bonus is gone:\n\n* **Year 2 Google Comp (without sign-on):**\n  * Base ($240k) + Bonus ($48k) + RSU ($270k) = **~$558,000** base compensation.\n  * Adding your first year of stacking refresh (~$50k\u2013$60k vesting) brings Year 2 to **~$610,000 \u2013 $620,000**.\n* **Comparison to Meta:**\n  * Meta also stacks refreshes. If you stay at Meta and perform well, your Year 2 comp would likely be in the **$540,000 \u2013 $580,000** range.\n* **Bottom line on money:** You lock in a guaranteed **$230k+ cash/equity premium in Year 1**, and an ongoing run-rate that is slightly above or roughly equal to Meta in Years 2\u20134.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Non-Financial Factors: Meta IC6 vs. Google L6\n\nSince the financial upside is now high enough to justify a move, the decision comes down to **scope, culture, and career trajectory**:\n\n| Factor | Meta (IC6 PM) | Google (L6 PM) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Pace & Execution** | Fast execution, bias for action, high individual ownership, intense bi-annual PSC / performance reviews. | More consensus-driven, matrixed stakeholder approvals, longer release cycles, less daily fire-drill pressure. |\n| **Promotion to L7 / IC7** | Very difficult, but path is clear if you deliver quantifiable metric impact. | High bar, notoriously slow promo process, heavily dependent on cross-functional sponsor packets. |\n| **Resume Value** | Having both **Meta and Google** at Senior PM level gives you maximum prestige and flexibility for future VP/CPO or startup Head of Product exits. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. Should You Take It?\n\n#### **Take the Offer IF:**\n1. **You want to bank a guaranteed ~$760k\u2013$840k in Year 1.** An extra $200k in cash is real money that derisks the move entirely.\n2. **You want the resume badge.** Having Meta *and* Google Senior PM on your CV makes you top-tier for almost any future tech leadership role.\n3. **You are open to a culture shift** toward broader stakeholder management, larger-scale product governance, and generally better work-life balance compared to Meta's aggressive review cycles.\n\n#### **Stay at Meta IF:**\n1. **You are within 12\u201318 months of an IC7 promotion.** Switching resets your promo clock completely. An IC7 at Meta out-earns an L6 at Google significantly ($700k\u2013$900k+ ongoing).\n2. **You thrive strictly on Meta's fast, high-autonomy culture** and get frustrated by cross-team alignment, legal/policy reviews, and slower decision velocity.\n\n---\n\n### Next Step / Recommendation\n\n**Accept the offer (subject to team fit).** \n\nGoogle met your financial friction point by fully covering your unvested equity with $200k cash and matching your base. Make sure you are comfortable with the specific team, manager, and charter at Google SF; if the team is solid, this is an excellent career package to sign.",
